%S A397453 5,20,45,80,145,580,949,1305,1649,2320,3625,4901,5220,7105,9280,11745,
%T A397453 14500,17545,19604,20880,24505,28420,31025,32625,37120,41905,44109,
%U A397453 46980,52345,54805,58000,63945,70180,76705,78416,83520,109189,122525,124100,166465,176436,219220,240149,244565
%N A397453 Numbers k such that, writing m = floor(sqrt(k)), both k-m^2 and k+m^2 are perfect squares.
%C A397453 These are E. P. Stein numbers in the impartial Epstein's put or take a square game. They return a remoteness value of 2 and are also all P-positions. There are infinitely many terms, and every term is of the form d^2*(u^4 + 4*v^4), where u is odd, gcd(u, v) = 1, and (u^2-2*v^2)^2 < 4*u*v.
%C A397453 It appears that the sequence grows at an asymptotic rate approximately equal to x^0.25*log(x).
%H A397453 Cameron Rampell, <a href="/A397453/b397453.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..125</a>
%H A397453 J. Steuding and K. Volkert, <a href="https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/tg2/tg2.Abstract.html">A Diophantine Problem Related to a Nim-like Game</a>, INTEGERS, 19 (2019).
%e A397453 20 is a term because floor(sqrt(20))^2 = 16, and 20 - 16 = 4 and 20 + 16 = 36 are both perfect squares.
%t A397453 Select[Range[250000],IntegerQ[Sqrt[#-(m=Floor[Sqrt[#]])^2]] && IntegerQ[Sqrt[#+m^2]] &] (* _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 04 2026 *)
%o A397453 (PARI) isok(k) = my(m=sqrtint(k)); issquare(k-m^2) && issquare(k+m^2); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 02 2026
%Y A397453 Cf. A005240, A005241, A048760.
